    description: "## Statement (also in `fields.statement`)\n\nWhen a Symbol is written into the graph, the identity fields attached to it (stable_id, fingerprint, canonical_name) must either uniquely identify the Symbol within its compilation unit AND carry the scope they're documented to cover, OR be explicitly marked as not applicable to the Symbol's kind. Half-populated identity fields, formulas with high collision rates, and docstrings that oversell field coverage are violated forms.\n\n## Parent\n\n`META: Symbol identity must be honest about its uncertainty across the pipeline.` (Super-META covering all five pipeline stages — write / read / edge / provenance / representation.) This sub-META is the **write-side** branch.\n\n## Members\n\n- `INV-fusus` (P2 violated) — Python `stable_id` formula too coarse. 91% collision rate on hypergumbo self-analysis: 26,742 of 29,367 nodes are in collision groups. Five distinct `@dataclass`-decorated classes (`Symbol`, `Edge`, `Span`, `AnalysisRun`, `ExternalRef`) all share `stable_id=sha256:1e29284a8c92ef81`. Their `to_dict` methods cascade-collide on the same id. Root cause at `py.py:1526` — `_compute_stable_id` hashes only `kind:param_count:arity_flags:decorators:containing_stable_id`, which is identical across @dataclass classes in the same module.\n- `INV-sotiv` (P2 violated) — Symbol.stable_id never populated for kind in {variable, module, export, dependency, project, interface, type} and 99.4% of `kind=file` nodes. 1981 of 32253 nodes silently lack stable_id on self-analysis. The producer's kind-gate is too narrow.\n- `WI-fanun` (P3 todo_hard) — Symbol.fingerprint mostly null. 0.3% populated on self-analysis; only manifest analyzers (toml-v1, json-v1) set it. Source-code symbols always null despite `fingerprint_scheme` declared at top level. Either populate for source-code symbols or rename to manifest-scope.\n- `WI-luvig` (P4 todo_hard) — Symbol.canonical_name docstring oversells the field. The IR docstring claims \"fully qualified name (e.g., 'mymodule.MyClass.method')\" but mainstream analyzers (Python/JS/Go/Rust/Java/C++/Ruby) leave it None because their `name` is already qualified. Only niche analyzers (nix, hlsl, asm, capnp, ada, fish, verilog, powershell, css) populate. Documented as \"correct-by-construction\" — the fix is the docstring, not the data.\n\n## The shared question\n\nAll four items are facets of \"what does an analyzer write to identify a Symbol, and is that identity claim honest?\" The shapes diverge — `INV-fusus` is about formula collision, `INV-sotiv` is about formula coverage, `WI-fanun` is about field scope, `WI-luvig` is about field documentation — but the architectural conversation they share is the **write-side identity contract**: which fields claim what kind of identity, for which kinds, with what guarantees.\n\n## Why an architectural decision, not four independent fixes\n\n`INV-fusus` and `INV-sotiv` are two failure modes of the same `_compute_stable_id` function: under-fires for some kinds (`INV-sotiv`), over-collides for others (`INV-fusus`). Fixing one without thinking about the other risks shipping a fix for `INV-sotiv` that uses the same too-coarse formula.\n\n`WI-fanun` and `WI-luvig` each ask the same scope question: which kinds is this field supposed to cover, and what do we do for the others? Those decisions are coupled — if `fingerprint` is \"manifest-only\" (the documented-scope path), then `stable_id` and `canonical_name` are doing the source-code work, and their kind-coverage gaps (`INV-sotiv`, `WI-luvig`) become more load-bearing.\n\n## Closure criterion\n\nFor each of (stable_id, fingerprint, canonical_name): an explicit per-kind contract is declared somewhere reachable from the field's docstring AND every analyzer's actual population behavior conforms (positive test). Half-populated fields either get producers for the missing kinds OR get the docstring/schema updated to reflect their actual scope.\n\n## Related but separate\n\n- Sibling sub-META under same super-META: `INV-fosab` (read-side / lookup-disambiguation). Read-side calibrated-confidence approach (`INV-rovuj` flagship) is fundamentally the same shape as what a write-side honesty mechanism would look like for unresolvable cases.\n- Sibling under same super-META: `INV-zuhub` (edge-side fallback contract)."  # 314e
    fields:  # 314e
      statement: "When a Symbol is written into the graph, the identity fields attached to it (stable_id, fingerprint, canonical_name) must either uniquely identify the Symbol within its compilation unit AND carry the scope they are documented to cover, OR be explicitly marked as not applicable to the Symbol kind. Half-populated identity fields, formulas with high collision rates, and docstrings that oversell field coverage are violated forms."  # 314e
      root_cause: "No coherent write-side identity contract exists. _compute_stable_id has too-coarse inputs (INV-fusus: 91% collision rate) and too-narrow kind gates (INV-sotiv: 7 kinds + 99.4% of files never get one). Symbol.fingerprint is declared at the top-level schema but only manifest analyzers populate it (WI-fanun: 0.3% on source-code symbols). Symbol.canonical_name docstring claims fully-qualified names but mainstream analyzers leave it None because their name field is already qualified (WI-luvig: docs oversell). These are facets of one architectural question — which identity fields claim what kind of identity, for which Symbol kinds, with what guarantees — that has never been settled in one place."  # 314e
